          <article class="lesson-article">

            <div class="card">
              <h2><span class="lesson-article__number">1</span> The Story of ASL</h2>
              <p>
                Long before ASL had a name, there was a place where it didn't
                need one. On Martha's Vineyard, starting in the 1700s, a
                hereditary form of deafness ran so deep through the island's
                population that much of the town, hearing and Deaf alike,
                grew up signing as a matter of course. Deaf islanders farmed,
                fished, argued town politics, and told jokes in sign
                alongside their hearing neighbors, in a community where being
                Deaf was closer to an ordinary trait than a barrier. Martha's
                Vineyard Sign Language never became a widely-known language
                in its own right, but it fed directly into what came next.
              </p>
              <p>
                In 1817, Thomas Hopkins Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc founded
                the American School for the Deaf in Hartford, Connecticut,
                the first permanent school for Deaf students in the United
                States. Clerc was Deaf himself, educated in France, and
                fluent in French Sign Language (LSF). At the school, LSF
                mixed with Martha's Vineyard Sign Language and the many home
                sign systems Deaf students already brought with them from
                across New England. Out of that mixing, over the generations
                that followed, American Sign Language took shape, a
                language born from a community, not designed by any single
                person.
              </p>
              <h3>Proving it was a language at all</h3>
              <p>
                For most of the 20th century, sign languages were widely
                dismissed, even by well-meaning educators, as gesture, or
                pantomime, or a simplified stand-in for "real" language. That
                changed in the 1960s, when linguist William Stokoe published
                research showing ASL has everything a spoken language has:
                its own phonology (handshape, location, and movement,
                functioning the way sounds do in speech), its own grammar,
                its own way of building complex meaning. It was the first
                rigorous linguistic case that ASL wasn't broken English. It
                was its own language, and the argument reshaped how
                linguists, educators, and eventually the public understood
                sign language everywhere, not only in the U.S.
              </p>
              <h3>A community that spoke up for itself</h3>
              <p>
                In March 1988, Gallaudet University, the world's only
                university built specifically for Deaf and hard-of-hearing
                students, announced a new president. Every finalist for the
                job was Deaf except the one the board picked. Students shut
                the campus down in protest for a week, and "Deaf President
                Now" became a rallying cry that reached national news. The
                board reversed course, and I. King Jordan became Gallaudet's
                first Deaf president. The protest is remembered as a turning
                point for Deaf pride and self-determination, proof that the
                Deaf community wasn't just the subject of decisions made
                about it, but a community capable of making its own.
              </p>
              <p>
                Today, ASL is the primary language for a large community of
                Deaf and hard-of-hearing people across the United States and
                Canada. Estimates commonly range from several hundred
                thousand to over a million users, though sign language use
                is notoriously hard to survey precisely. It's taught in
                thousands of schools and universities, recognized as a
                foreign language for academic credit in many U.S. states,
                and, like any living language, still changing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h2><span class="lesson-article__number">2</span> A Few Things Worth Knowing</h2>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Deaf culture is a culture, not just an absence of hearing.</strong> Many Deaf people describe Deafness as an identity and community, not a deficit to be fixed.</li>
                <li><strong>ASL isn't signed English.</strong> Its grammar and word order are its own. A fluent ASL sentence doesn't map word-for-word onto an English one.</li>
                <li><strong>Facial expression is grammar, not just emotion.</strong> Eyebrow position and head tilt can be the difference between a statement and a question in ASL.</li>
                <li><strong>Learning ASL is a long-term skill, like any language</strong>: a little consistent practice beats an occasional long session, which is exactly what the path after this page is built around.</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="text-muted mt-4">
                This is intentionally a short list. For the full walkthrough (Deaf etiquette, why ASL is worth learning, and how visual
                communication actually works), see
                <a href="../pages/intro-to-asl.html">the full Introduction to ASL guide</a>.
              </p>
            </div>

          </article>

          <div class="text-center mt-8 mb-8">
            <a href="learn.html" class="btn btn--primary">Continue to the Learning Path →</a>
          </div>

        </div>
