      <div class="text-center mb-8">
        <p class="section__eyebrow">Module 1</p>
        <h1>Introduction to ASL</h1>
        <p class="text-muted mt-4">
          Before you start practicing handshapes in front of the camera, it helps
          to know what ASL actually is, who uses it, and how to communicate
          respectfully with the community it belongs to.
        </p>
      </div>

      <article class="lesson-article">

        <div class="card">
          <h2><span class="lesson-article__number">1</span> What is ASL?</h2>
          <p>
            American Sign Language is a complete, natural language, not a
            hand-signed version of English. It has its own grammar, its own
            word order, and its own way of building meaning that doesn't map
            word-for-word onto spoken or written English. A sentence signed in
            ASL and the same sentence written in English can look completely
            different in structure, even when they mean exactly the same
            thing.
          </p>

          <h3>A short history</h3>
          <p>
            Modern ASL traces back to 1817, when the American School for the
            Deaf was founded in Hartford, Connecticut, by Thomas Hopkins
            Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc, a Deaf educator who had signed in
            French Sign Language (LSF) his whole life. At the school, LSF
            blended with the various home sign systems Deaf students already
            used, and ASL grew from that mix over the generations that
            followed. It's genuinely its own language, with roots in both
            French Sign Language and American Deaf communities, not a
            simplified version of anything else.
          </p>

          <h3>Why it matters</h3>
          <p>
            For a long time, sign languages were dismissed as simple gesture
            systems rather than real languages. That changed with the
            linguistic research of William Stokoe in the 1960s, which showed
            ASL has every structural property a spoken language has:
            grammar, syntax, the ability to express anything a spoken language
            can. Today it's recognized as a full language in its own right,
            and it's the primary language for a large community of Deaf and
            hard-of-hearing people across the U.S. and Canada.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h2><span class="lesson-article__number">2</span> Deaf Awareness</h2>
          <p>
            The Deaf community is a linguistic and cultural group, not simply
            "people who can't hear." Many Deaf people describe Deafness as an
            identity and a culture they're part of, not a condition to be
            fixed, worth keeping in mind as you learn a language that belongs
            to that community.
          </p>

          <h3>Basic communication etiquette</h3>
          <ul class="etiquette-list">
            <li><strong>Get attention respectfully.</strong> A wave in someone's
              line of sight, a light tap on the shoulder, or a gentle tap on
              the table works, not shouting or grabbing.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep eye contact during conversation.</strong> In a
              visual language, looking away is like turning your back on
              someone mid-sentence.</li>
            <li><strong>Don't assume lipreading works.</strong> It's difficult
              and unreliable even for people who are skilled at it. Many
              English sounds look identical on the lips.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep your hands and face visible.</strong> Covering
              your mouth or signing space makes you hard to understand, the
              same way mumbling would.</li>
            <li><strong>Be patient, not performative.</strong> If you're still
              learning, it's fine to write things down or ask someone to
              repeat something. That's normal, not rude.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Understanding Deaf culture</h3>
          <p>
            Deaf culture includes its own humor, storytelling traditions, art,
            and history, much of it centered around Deaf schools and
            organizations, which have long served as community hubs. One
            useful distinction: a "medical" view treats deafness purely as
            hearing loss to be corrected, while the cultural view many Deaf
            people hold sees Deaf identity as something to be valued, not
            something to fix. Both perspectives exist in the world; approaching
            the community with curiosity and respect, rather than pity, goes a
            long way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h2><span class="lesson-article__number">3</span> Benefits of Learning ASL</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Communication accessibility</strong>: you can talk
              directly with Deaf and hard-of-hearing people without relying on
              an interpreter or passing notes back and forth.</li>
            <li><strong>Social inclusion</strong>: even a little ASL lowers
              the barrier for a Deaf person to be included in a conversation,
              rather than left to lip-read or wait for someone else to
              translate.</li>
            <li><strong>Career opportunities</strong>: interpreting, Deaf
              education, healthcare, social work, and customer-facing roles
              increasingly value ASL fluency as a real, in-demand skill.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h2><span class="lesson-article__number">4</span> Introduction to Visual Communication</h2>
          <p>
            ASL linguists describe every sign as a combination of five
            parameters: <strong>handshape</strong>, <strong>location</strong>,
            <strong>movement</strong>, <strong>palm orientation</strong>, and
            <strong>non-manual markers</strong> (everything happening above
            the hands). Change any one of these and you can change the
            meaning of the sign entirely. It's not just "wave your hands
            around."
          </p>

          <h3>Facial expressions</h3>
          <p>
            In ASL, facial expression isn't just emotional flavor. It's
            grammar. Raised eyebrows can turn a statement into a yes/no
            question; furrowed brows often signal a "wh-" question (who,
            what, where). The same handshape with a different expression can
            carry a different grammatical meaning entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            Facial expression also carries emotional tone, the same way your
            voice does when you're speaking. It's not just decoration on
            top of the sign. A tense, angry face paired with a sign usually
            means the signer feels negatively about what they're saying;
            a soft, open expression on that same sign can soften it or even
            flip the feeling behind it entirely. It's less a strict rule and
            more like tone of voice: the words (or signs) carry the message,
            and the face carries how the signer feels about it, so reading
            the face is often just as important as reading the hands.
          </p>

          <h3>Body language</h3>
          <p>
            Signers use body shift and lean to mark different characters or
            perspectives when telling a story, leaning one way for "what I
            said," the other way for "what they said." Space itself carries
            meaning too: where you place a sign relative to your body can
            establish who or what you're referring to later in the
            conversation.
          </p>

          <h3>Hand movements</h3>
          <p>
            This is the part LinguaWave's camera is built to recognize:
            handshape, location, and movement together. It's also why this
            app tracks full motion sequences for many signs, not just a
            single snapshot: the movement itself often carries part of the
            sign's meaning, not just its starting handshape.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h2><span class="lesson-article__number">5</span> ASL Learning Tips</h2>
          <h3>Practice techniques</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Practice in front of a mirror or camera, like this app, so
              you can actually see what a native signer would see.</li>
            <li>Fingerspell something every day, even just your own name or a
              word you see around you.</li>
            <li>Watch native Deaf signers when you can (video, in person, or
              Deaf-led content online) to pick up natural rhythm and
              non-manual markers that are hard to learn from text alone.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Learning strategies</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Consistency beats cramming: a little practice most days sticks
              far better than one long session a week.</li>
            <li>Learn in categories, the way this app is organized (family,
              places, numbers, and so on). Grouped vocabulary is easier to
              retain than a random list.</li>
            <li>Expect ASL grammar to feel unfamiliar at first. It's a
              genuinely different language, not reordered English. Give
              yourself room to learn it as its own thing.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </article>

      <div class="text-center mt-8">
        <a href="learn.html?unit=alphabet" class="btn btn--primary">Continue to the Alphabet →</a>
      </div>

    </div>
