              getAllMissions(), getMissionProgress(), getItemCompletedAt(),
              getStreakSummary(), getHeartsState(), getCategoryGroups() —
              plus window.LWData for sign titles. No hardcoded numbers.

    PHASE 3 (this revision, on top of the Phase 1 "Learning Progress
    Center" redesign) — "make it feel distinctly LinguaWave," per
    progressUI_guidelines.docx's 3-phase plan. Nothing structural
    from Phase 1 moved; this only adds two new pieces (both real-data-
    gated, hidden until there's something true to put in them — see
    their own markup comments below) and reworks the chapter timeline's
    connecting line into an animated wavy path:
      - Completion celebration banner (§11 of the guidelines doc)
      - Sign Mastery grid, scoped to one chapter at a time (§ Phase 3
        "sign-level mastery visualization")
      - The chapter timeline's straight connector line -> a wavy SVG
        path that draws itself in on load, plus a "Just completed"
        pulse on a chapter finished in the last 24h (real
        getItemCompletedAt() timestamps, not a fabricated instant) —
        together, "animated learning journey" + "wave/path
        visualization between chapters."
      - Next Milestone -> "contextual recommendation": still the same
        card/heading by default, but js/progress-page.js's
        pickRecommendation() now checks review urgency and drop-off
        position first and swaps in a different heading/CTA when one
        of those is the more useful thing to surface right now.
    "Contextual recommendations" (the guideline doc's 5th Phase 3 item)
    was deliberately folded into the existing Next Milestone card
    rather than added as a new one — the doc's own closing note warns
    against making this page "feel like a game overloaded with
    badges," and a second box making basically the same kind of
    suggestion would do exactly that.

    DIFFERENCES FROM THE MOCKUP, ON PURPOSE (same "don't fabricate
    data that doesn't exist" discipline js/progress-page.js documents
    for the v1 page):
      - "Progress by Level" -> "Progress by Chapter". Missions has no
        basic/medium/intermediate level grouping of its own (that's a
        js/data.js/js/engine/progress.js concept) — it organizes
        missions into the 12 named chapters from getCategoryGroups().
        Chapters with zero live missions built yet are left out rather
        than shown as a fake "0%".
      - "Needs Review": UNLIKE pages/progress.html (which has no
        timestamps and has to fall back to "most recently practiced"),
        Missions DOES record a real completedAt per item
        (getItemCompletedAt(), added Phase 2) — so this section shows
        signs whose LESSON item was actually completed 2+ days ago, a
        genuine (if simple) due rule, not a fabricated one. If nothing
        is old enough yet, the panel says so instead of padding the
        list with a made-up "3 days ago".
      - Two extra stat tiles beyond the mockup's three (Streak, Hearts)
        — both are real Missions-only concepts (js/missions.js's streak
        and Hearts modules) already surfaced elsewhere in
        (dashboard.html, mission-overview.html); showing them
        here too keeps this page consistent with the rest of rather
        than omitting data that already exists. The Hearts tile shows a
        heart glyph + count ("♥ 3") and the Streak tile shows the day
        count beside this week's Mon–Sun strip (same cell styles as the
        #progress-consistency heatmap) — see renderHero() /
        buildStreakWeek() in js/progress-page.js. Clicking the Streak
        tile opens a dialog (flame, "N Day Streak", last 5 days as check
        boxes) built by openStreakDialog() in the same file; it is
        appended to <body> on demand, so nothing is needed in this markup.

    PHASE 2 : "Make progress feel personal" — four sections added below
