         narrower than every section around it and pushed its left edge off the shared
         grid. At full container width the two-column cards still hold ~65 characters a line. -->
  <section class="section section--sm" id="about-asl">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>About American Sign Language</h2>
      <div class="grid-2 mt-4">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What is ASL?</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">American Sign Language is a complete, natural language with its own grammar and syntax. It isn't a signed version of English. It's used primarily by Deaf communities across the US and parts of Canada.</p>
          <p class="text-muted">Its roots trace to 1817, when the American School for the Deaf opened in Hartford, Connecticut, founded by Thomas Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc, a Deaf teacher from France. ASL grew from French Sign Language blending with sign systems already used locally.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Why it matters</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">In the 1960s, linguist William Stokoe's research proved ASL has the full structure of a real language, a finding that reshaped how it's taught and recognized today.</p>
          <p class="text-muted">Learning it opens direct communication with the Deaf community, and shows up in fields like education, interpreting, and healthcare.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Deaf awareness &amp; etiquette</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">Many in the Deaf community see themselves as a linguistic and cultural group, not as having something to "fix." A few habits go a long way:</p>
          <ul class="etiquette-list text-muted">
            <li>Keep eye contact: it's how a signed conversation stays connected.</li>
            <li>Get attention with a wave or a light tap on the shoulder, not shouting.</li>
            <li>Face the person directly and keep your face visible; facial expression carries grammar, not just emotion.</li>
            <li>It's fine to ask to write something down instead of guessing. Don't fake understanding.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>How a sign works</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">Stokoe identified that every sign is built from a few parameters: handshape, location, movement, and palm orientation, plus facial expression, which changes meaning the way tone of voice does in speech.</p>
          <p class="text-muted">That's also why LinguaWave's camera tracks motion and position rather than grading a single still frame.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
