| 09-21 (Guided tour: "Replay all guides" button replaces the Guide popups switch; Skip becomes "Skip all") | **(1) Switch removed, replay can no longer be blocked.** `pages/settings.html` has one row, "Guided tour" with a "Replay all guides" button; `#pref-guides` and the `showGuides` field are gone from `js/settings-page.js` (it never reads or writes it now). Clicking the button calls `LWTour.resetAll()` (forgets every guide plus any Skip all) and opens `dashboard.html?tour=1`, so the tour visibly starts instead of just changing a label; the other pages' guides play on their next visit. `isEnabled()`/`setEnabled()` and `SKIP_ENDS_ALL_GUIDES` are deleted from `js/tour.js`, so nothing gates auto-start except what the account has seen. **Legacy switch:** a device that had the old switch OFF would otherwise stay silent with no way back, and a stale `showGuides:false` would have re-dismissed the tour right after a replay. `takeLegacySwitch()` deletes the field on the first guide page (or inside `resetAll()`); if it was off, that is honoured once as Skip all. **(2) Skip / Skip all.** Skip still closes only the current page's guide (status `skipped`). After two skipped guides, every later automatic guide shows "Skip all" (decided when the guide opens, so it is the same on every step; `aria-label` "Skip all guides", plain Skip is "Skip this guide"), and pressing it sets one `__all__` flag on the account in `lw_tour_seen_v1`, so guides added later stay dismissed too; a note points to Settings and the help button. Esc does what the visible button does. **Counting rules, on purpose:** only Skip pressed on an automatic guide counts. Finishing a guide, leaving the page mid-guide (was recorded as `skipped`, now `closed`) and skipping a guide opened from the "?" button or `?tour=1` do not, and a recorded skip is never downgraded by replaying that guide, so reloading a page or using the help button cannot push a learner toward Skip all. Manual runs always show plain "Skip". The count is derived from stored statuses, per account (two accounts on one device count separately). `lwtour:end` now reports `done | skipped | dismissed | closed`. `css/tour.css`: `.lw-tour__link` is `nowrap` so the longer label stays on one line. Verified in headless Chromium with Firebase/CDNs stubbed: 75 assertions (settings markup, Skip, Skip, Skip all across dashboard/learn/progress, nothing auto-starts afterwards, help button after Skip all, Esc, done/reload not counted, per-account counts, Replay from a dismissed state, all three legacy-switch cases, one-line label with no overflow at 1440/560/421/390/320 in both themes, Tab focus loop). NOT covered: a real device, a screen reader, the camera page with live MediaPipe, mission-overview/lesson/mastery-quiz guides (engine path is the same, not re-run). Supersedes the Skip and Settings sentences of the 09-21 "Guided tour: hint popups" row above. | `js/tour.js`, `js/settings-page.js`, `pages/settings.html`, `css/tour.css`, `AI_MEMORY.md` |
| 09-21 (Guided tour: the "?" help button removed everywhere) | Requested follow-up to the row above. `injectHelpButton()` and its call are deleted from `js/tour.js`, and every `.tour-help` rule (base, hover, focus, the 420px hide, the print rule) is deleted from `css/tour.css`; no HTML page ever carried the button (it was injected into `.app-sidebar__foot` / `.navbar__user` at runtime), so no page markup changed. **What that changes:** the only in-app way to see guides again is now Settings > "Replay all guides" (plus `?tour=1` in a URL); a single page's guide can no longer be replayed from that page. The two skip notes no longer mention a help button and now point to Settings. `start(id, {manual: true})` is kept on `window.LWTour` for programmatic use, and still shows plain "Skip" and counts nothing toward "Skip all", but nothing in the UI calls it now. The `questions` icon in `js/icons.js` was only used by that button among tour code; left in place since it is part of the shared 126-icon set. Verified in headless Chromium (Firebase/CDNs stubbed): 83 assertions, including no `.tour-help` element and no "Show the guide for this page" label on the dashboard, learn, progress, mission-overview, lesson, mastery-quiz, camera-practice and settings pages. NOT covered: a real device or screen reader. Supersedes every "?" help-button statement in the two 09-21 guided-tour rows above. | `js/tour.js`, `css/tour.css`, `AI_MEMORY.md` |
| 09-26 (Auth modal — floating labels on Log In/Sign Up, then a width regression fix, then the tab switch smoothed) | Three related passes on the same `#signin-modal` in `index.html`, done in one session. **(1) Floating labels.** Added a material-style floating-label treatment to all 5 fields (login email/password; register name/email/password) — the technique needs the `<input>` before its `<label>` (sibling-selector order) plus `placeholder=" "` (lets `:not(:placeholder-shown)` detect "has a value"), so both were reordered/added in `index.html`. New rules in `css/auth.css` are scoped under `.auth-form .form-group`/`.form-input`/`label`, reusing the existing class names rather than introducing new ones, so `pages/feedback.html`'s separate `.form-input` textarea (no `.form-group` wrapper, outside `.auth-form`) is untouched. Notch/focus color reads off this app's own tokens (`--clr-accent`, `--clr-surface`, `--clr-text-muted`) instead of a hardcoded pair, so it follows whichever theme is active. The old inline example placeholders (`you@example.com`, `Min. 8 characters`, etc.) are gone — the floating label now sits where they did; not restored as helper text, not asked for. **(2) Width regression, same session.** Switching `.form-group` from `flex; flex-direction:column` to `position:relative` (needed for the label overlay) dropped the old flex-stretch behavior that had been the only thing making the inputs full-width — `.form-input` itself never set its own `width`, so they silently fell back to the browser's narrow default. Fixed with an explicit `width:100%` on `.auth-form .form-input`. **(3) Log In/Sign Up switch smoothed, per explicit follow-up that the switch "feels fast."** Two separate things were fixed, not one: (a) the login/register forms previously snapped `display:none↔flex` instantly with no transition at all — now crossfade via `.auth-form--fade-out`/`.auth-form--fade-in-start` (opacity + small `translateY`, outgoing fades+lifts 160ms then swaps `display`, incoming fades+settles 250ms using the existing `--dur-base`/`--ease` tokens), driven from `showAuthTab()` in `index.html` with a `setTimeout` bridging the two and a guard against rapid re-clicks stacking transitions. (b) separately, per a follow-up clarifying the complaint was actually the blue tab highlight: `tab-login`/`tab-register` no longer each fade their own `background-color` in/out (both firing on every click read as a flash, not motion, at the old 150ms `--dur-fast`) — replaced with one absolutely-positioned `#auth-tabs-indicator` pill that physically slides between them via `transform: translateX()`, sized/positioned in JS (`positionAuthTabsIndicator()`, uses `offsetLeft`/`offsetWidth` against the first tab) and called with `animate=false` right after the modal becomes visible (`openAuthModal()`) since it can't be measured while `display:none`, and `animate=true` on every real tab click; the buttons themselves now only transition text color. Delivered as an updated project zip each pass, unzip-into-root. Not otherwise verified in a real browser this session (no headless/Playwright environment available here) — reasoned through against the real markup/CSS/JS, not click-tested. | `index.html`, `css/auth.css`, `AI_MEMORY.md` |
